{#
  One real <table> per listing, with pagination, free-text search, and four multi-value filter
  dimensions -- category, mod, tag, and description (see `descriptionAttrs` below) -- each rendered as a button that opens a small checkbox
  panel (see datatable.js) populated from whatever `data-category`/`data-mod`/`data-tags` values
  are actually present on the rows. Checking several boxes in one dimension matches rows with ANY
  of those values (OR within a dimension); the three dimensions (plus the free-text search) are
  ANDed together, same as before. A dimension's whole button+panel widget is hidden only if no row
  has a value for it at all (a single distinct value still shows it, since that's what makes it
  start working immediately for free once a 2nd value appears). `data-tags` is space-separated and
  matched per-token (exact match, not the free-text `?q=` substring search) -- a row can carry
  several tags and match on any one of them. Every active value across all three dimensions also
  renders as a removable chip below the controls row, regardless of which dimension it came from.
  All filters sync to URL query params as comma-separated lists (`?category=rock,ore`), numbered
  q2/category2/mod2/tag2 etc for the 2nd+ datatable on the same page, so a filtered view can be
  bookmarked/shared and survives back/forward navigation -- see datatable.js. Only one filter
  panel is ever open at a time, across every dimension and every datatable instance on the page --
  opening one closes any other that was open, rather than letting several stack up.
  Pass `headers` for the <thead> and have the caller emit
  <tr data-search="..." data-category="..." data-mod="..." data-mod-label="..." data-tags="...">
  <td>...</td>...</tr> rows (any of these data-* attributes can be omitted when not meaningful
  for that listing). `data-mod` is the modid the filter matches on; `data-mod-label` (optional) is
  the human-readable name shown in the panel instead (e.g. "BetterRuins" instead of
  "betterruins") -- see the `modName` filter in `eleventy.config.js`, used as
  `{{ generated.mods | modName(x.sourceMod) }}`. Falls back to the raw modid when omitted.
  When the caller produces zero rows, the whole widget (including any surrounding heading the page
  itself wraps around it) should be skipped by the page template -- this macro itself always
  renders the table shell since it doesn't know the row count up front.
  Every header is clickable to sort the (filtered) rows by that column's cell text (numeric-aware;
  click again to reverse, a 3rd click returns to unsorted) -- see datatable.js, no data-* opt-in
  needed, this applies to every datatable automatically. The sorted column/direction also syncs to
  the URL (`?sort=<colIndex>&dir=asc|desc`, numbered the same way as the filters), the same
  bookmarkable treatment the filters get.
#}
{% macro datatable(pageSize, placeholder, headers) %}
<div class="datatable" data-page-size="{{ pageSize or 50 }}">
  <div class="datatable-controls">
    <input type="search" class="datatable-search" placeholder="{{ placeholder or 'Search...' }}" aria-label="{{ placeholder or 'Search' }}">
    <div class="datatable-filter" data-filter-dimension="category">
      <button type="button" class="datatable-filter-toggle" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Category</button>
      <div class="datatable-filter-panel" hidden></div>
    </div>
    <div class="datatable-filter" data-filter-dimension="mod">
      <button type="button" class="datatable-filter-toggle" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Mod</button>
      <div class="datatable-filter-panel" hidden></div>
    </div>
    <div class="datatable-filter" data-filter-dimension="tag">
      <button type="button" class="datatable-filter-toggle" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Tags</button>
      <div class="datatable-filter-panel" hidden></div>
    </div>
    <div class="datatable-filter" data-filter-dimension="description">
      <button type="button" class="datatable-filter-toggle" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false">Description</button>
      <div class="datatable-filter-panel" hidden></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="datatable-active-filters" data-datatable-active-filters></div>
  <table>
    {% if headers %}<thead><tr>{% for h in headers %}<th class="datatable-sortable" data-col="{{ loop.index0 }}"><span class="datatable-th-label">{{ h | safe }}</span></th>{% endfor %}</tr></thead>{% endif %}
    <tbody data-datatable-items>
      {{ caller() }}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="datatable-pagination" data-datatable-pagination></div>
</div>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The row attributes for the "Description" filter dimension: whether this row's thing has a
  localized description (an itemdesc-/blockdesc-/characterdesc-/traitdesc- lang string). Emit on
  every row of a listing whose content type *can* have one (pass the raw description, or
  undefined) -- `<tr data-...{{ dt.descriptionAttrs(x.description) }}>`. Filters as
  `?description=yes|no`; listings that never emit it don't show the Description button at all.
#}
{% macro descriptionAttrs(description) %} data-description="{{ 'yes' if description else 'no' }}" data-description-label="{{ 'Has description' if description else 'No description' }}"{% endmacro %}
